topics.lessonMarkdown:
# 5.1 useEffect & Cleanups

## First question: what is an effect?

A React render should describe the UI.

Sometimes your component also needs to synchronize with something outside that render calculation:

- timer
- browser document title
- network request
- event listener
- subscription
- external widget

That is where `useEffect` is useful.

---

## 1. Basic shape

```jsx
import { useEffect } from "react";

useEffect(() => {
  // side-effect work
});
```

A dependency array controls when the effect is re-synchronized:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  // ...
}, []);
```

The empty array means the effect does not re-run because of ordinary state/prop dependency changes after mounting. Note that development Strict Mode can intentionally run setup/cleanup more than once to help reveal bugs.

---

## 2. Timer example

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => {
    console.log("tick");
  }, 1000);

  return () => {
    clearInterval(timer);
  };
}, []);
```

Why cleanup?

Because an interval keeps running until it is stopped.

Without cleanup you can create:

- duplicate timers
- memory leaks
- unexpected updates
- code that keeps running after the component is gone

---

## 3. Digital clock

```jsx
const [time, setTime] = useState(
  new Date().toLocaleTimeString()
);

useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => {
    setTime(new Date().toLocaleTimeString());
  }, 1000);

  return () => clearInterval(timer);
}, []);
```

The render reads `time`.

The effect updates `time` every second.

The component renders again.

---

## 4. Dependency array mental model

Consider:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("runs after every render");
});
```

No dependency array.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("runs after initial mount");
}, []);
```

Empty dependency array.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("runs when roomId changes");
}, [roomId]);
```

One dependency.

The exact behavior also interacts with development Strict Mode, so do not use mount-only wording as a guarantee that setup runs exactly once in all environments.

---

## 5. Cleanup happens before the next setup when dependencies change

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const connection = connect(roomId);

  return () => {
    connection.disconnect();
  };
}, [roomId]);
```

When `roomId` changes, React cleans up the previous connection before synchronizing with the new one.

This is a powerful pattern for subscriptions and external resources.

---

## 6. `useEffect` is not "do everything here"

A common beginner mistake is putting ordinary calculations into effects.

Instead of:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  setTotal(price * quantity);
}, [price, quantity]);
```

you can usually calculate:

```jsx
const total = price * quantity;
```

Use an effect when you are synchronizing with something outside React.

---

## 7. Example: document title

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Cart (${count})`;
}, [count]);
```

When the cart count changes, React synchronizes the browser tab title.

---

## 8. Example: event listener cleanup

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  function handleResize() {
    console.log(window.innerWidth);
  }

  window.addEventListener("resize", handleResize);

  return () => {
    window.removeEventListener("resize", handleResize);
  };
}, []);
```

The exact same pattern appears:

```text
start subscription
       ↓
component active
       ↓
cleanup subscription
```

---

## 9. Fetching data: the basic pattern

A simple introductory pattern is:

```jsx
const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

useEffect(() => {
  fetch("/api/users")
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => setUsers(data))
    .finally(() => setLoading(false));
}, []);
```

Real applications also need error handling, cancellation or race protection where appropriate, loading states, and a strategy for caching/revalidation.

---

## 10. Cleanup and memory

Cleanup is not just a performance trick. It expresses ownership:

> "While this component needs this external resource, keep it active. When it no longer needs it, stop it."

That concept will help you later with subscriptions, WebSockets, observers, intervals, and timers.

---

## Complete example

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

export default function App() {
  const [time, setTime] = useState(
    new Date().toLocaleTimeString()
  );

  useEffect(() => {
    const interval = setInterval(() => {
      setTime(new Date().toLocaleTimeString());
    }, 1000);

    return () => {
      clearInterval(interval);
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>{time}</h1>
      <p>Clock is running.</p>
    </main>
  );
}
```

---

## Common mistakes

- Forgetting cleanup for ongoing resources.
- Leaving dependencies out when the effect reads reactive values.
- Using effects for values that can be calculated during render.
- Starting a timer or subscription in the component body.
- Assuming an effect with `[]` means "exactly once under every circumstance".

Further reading:
- React: Synchronizing with Effects — https://react.dev/learn/synchronizing-with-effects
- React: Lifecycle of Reactive Effects — https://react.dev/learn/lifecycle-of-reactive-effects
- W3Schools: React useEffect — https://www.w3schools.com/react/react_useeffect.asp
- GeeksforGeeks: Fetching Data Using useEffect and useState — https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/reactjs/fetching-data-from-an-api-with-useeffect-and-usestate-hook/

---

## 11. `useRef`: remembering a value without causing a render

`useRef` is useful when you need a value that persists between renders but changing it should not itself trigger a re-render.

```jsx
import { useRef } from "react";

function App() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  const focusInput = () => {
    inputRef.current.focus();
  };

  return (
    <>
      <input ref={inputRef} />
      <button onClick={focusInput}>
        Focus input
      </button>
    </>
  );
}
```

The ref object stays the same between renders.

Before the input is connected:

```javascript
inputRef.current
```

may be `null`.

After React attaches the ref to the input, `current` points to the DOM element.

---

## `useRef` vs `useState`

Use state when changing the value should update the UI:

```javascript
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
```

Use a ref when you need to remember something without rendering a new UI value:

```javascript
const timerRef = useRef(null);
```

Common ref uses:

- focus an input
- store a timer ID
- remember a DOM node
- keep a mutable value across renders when UI does not depend on that change

Do not use refs as a replacement for normal application state.

